Replace the deprecated Qt calls of the Qt 6 style

The Qt 6 style called four members that Qt marks as deprecated. A build with
-Wall -Wextra printed six warning lines for three of them. The compiler stays
silent for the fourth, because the header defines that constructor as
defaulted.

Qt deprecated QMouseEvent::globalPos() in 6.0. The window manager calls
globalPosition().toPoint() now. The header defines the old member as that same
expression, so the drag point stays an integer point. The equality test and the
drag threshold then compare integers, as before.

Qt deprecated the QMouseEvent constructor of five arguments in 6.4. The two
synthetic events take the constructor with the global position now. The
deprecated constructor read that position from the cursor, so the calls pass
QCursor::pos(). An offscreen program shows that the position, the scene
position, the global position and the pointing device stay equal.

Qt deprecated QApplication::fontMetrics() in 6.0. The style calls
QFontMetrics(qApp->font()) now. The header names QFontMetricsF, which does not
convert to the return type of the function and which gives other values. The
new call gives the same height, ascent, descent and text advance.

Qt deprecated the QFontDatabase constructor in 6.0. The font helper called two
static members through one instance, and the instance held no state. The helper
calls the static functions now, and it drops the member, the forward
declaration and the delete.

The change sits on the branch work/qt6-deprecated-api of the local tree. The
fork wyn/qtcurve holds that branch. Merge request !11 on system/qtcurve holds
that branch as the source.

Drop this patch when upstream merges the change.

